+# The Daily Grind
+
+A responsive customer account experience with real, server-verified WebAuthn passkeys. Includes account registration, discoverable sign-in and conditional autofill, saved passkey names and removal, profile updates, communication preferences, and passkey reauthentication for sensitive changes.
+
+## Setup
+
+Requires Node.js 22.13+ (Node 24 recommended).
+
+```sh
+pnpm install
+pnpm build
+```
+
+To run the application when appropriate, `pnpm start` serves the homepage and account API on port 11222, or `$PORT`. The frontend bundle is committed so the start command does not need to build. A static-only server can display the design but cannot handle authentication.
+
+Set `APP_ORIGIN` to the **exact externally visible origin**, including a nonstandard port if used (for example `https://coffee.example.com`). Set `RP_ID` if the relying party domain should differ from the origin hostname (for example `example.com`). Both default to `http://localhost:$PORT` and its hostname for local development. Production origins must use HTTPS. Do not change the RP ID after issuing passkeys; existing credentials are scoped to that domain. TLS can terminate at a reverse proxy; forward requests without rewriting the browser's Origin header. The application never trusts Host or X-Forwarded-Host as its security configuration.
+
+## Account behavior
+
+- Register with a name and email; account creation completes only after passkey verification. The email is an **unverified account identifier**. No email service or email-based recovery is configured.
+- Sign in with a discoverable passkey, without entering an email. The optional email field enables native `webauthn` autofill on supporting browsers.
+- Signed-in customers can add, rename, or remove passkeys, edit their profile, and save communication preferences. Preferences are stored only; no marketing messages are sent.
+- Adding or changing passkeys and updating the profile request a fresh, account-constrained assertion with user verification required. Server-side authorization expires after five minutes. Reauthentication verifies that the credential belongs to the active account.
+- Removal of the last passkey is blocked. Customers should add a backup passkey or use a synced password manager; there is no administrative account recovery flow.
+- WebAuthn JSON serialization and client capability detection use `webauthn-polyfills`. Signal API calls are feature-detected and best effort. Vault synchronization runs when loading the passkey list, after removal, and after profile updates.
+- Provider labels and icons come from a small local snapshot of the [Passkey Developer AAGUID registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids). Unknown or zeroed AAGUIDs use a generic key icon and label. Provider metadata never authorizes access.
+
+## Storage and security
+
+SQLite persists account records, public credential keys, counters, timestamps, and expiring sessions in `.data/accounts.sqlite`. Mount `.data` as private persistent storage in deployment and back it up. Private credential keys and biometric data never reach this application. Session tokens are random, stored hashed, rotated after successful authentication, and sent in HttpOnly, SameSite cookies (Secure on HTTPS). API mutations require the configured Origin and a session-bound CSRF token. Challenges are single-use, expire after five minutes, and are bound to a session and ceremony type. Authentication validates origin, RP ID, signature, ownership, user handle, and counter through SimpleWebAuthn. Basic per-address request limiting is included.
+
+This is a single-process deployment. For horizontal scaling, use a shared production database and shared rate limiter. The session store and SQLite file must not be exposed as static files. The Node server serves an explicit allowlist of public assets.
+
+The menu and story are available from the homepage navigation. Location search, checkout, and outbound support are outside this account implementation; their UI explains the available functionality.
+
+## Validation
+
+`pnpm build` bundles the browser application and syntax-checks both backend files without importing or starting a server. No browser or server verification was performed for this change.
